[{"order":1,"type":"heading","text":"Spring Profiles: Managing Different Environments (Dev, Test, Prod)"},{"order":2,"type":"paragraph","text":"Spring Profiles provide a way to configure different application behaviors based on the environment in which the application is running. This is crucial for managing different configurations for development, testing, and production environments. Using profiles, you can easily switch between different configurations without modifying the core application code."},{"order":3,"type":"subheading","text":"Understanding Spring Profiles"},{"order":4,"type":"paragraph","text":"A Spring Profile is a named set of bean definitions that are registered only when the profile is active. You can activate one or more profiles at runtime, allowing you to customize the application context based on the environment."},{"order":5,"type":"subheading","text":"Defining Spring Profiles"},{"order":6,"type":"paragraph","text":"You can define profiles in several ways, including using annotations, XML configuration, or a combination of both. The most common approach in Spring Boot is using annotations."},{"order":7,"type":"subheading","text":"Using @Profile Annotation"},{"order":8,"type":"paragraph","text":"The `@Profile` annotation is used to indicate that a component or configuration class should be registered only when a specific profile (or profiles) is active."},{"order":9,"type":"code","text":"@Configuration
@Profile("dev")
public class DevConfig {

    @Bean
    public DataSource dataSource() {
        // Configure data source for development environment
        DriverManagerDataSource dataSource = new DriverManagerDataSource();
        dataSource.setDriverClassName("org.h2.Driver");
        dataSource.setUrl("jdbc:h2:mem:devdb");
        dataSource.setUsername("sa");
        dataSource.setPassword("");
        return dataSource;
    }
}"},{"order":10,"type":"paragraph","text":"In the example above, the `DevConfig` class and its associated `dataSource` bean will only be registered when the `dev` profile is active."},{"order":11,"type":"code","text":"@Configuration
@Profile("prod")
public class ProdConfig {

    @Bean
    public DataSource dataSource() {
        // Configure data source for production environment
        DriverManagerDataSource dataSource = new DriverManagerDataSource();
        dataSource.setDriverClassName("com.mysql.cj.jdbc.Driver");
        dataSource.setUrl("jdbc://production-db-url");
        dataSource.setUsername("prod_user");
        dataSource.setPassword("prod_password");
        return dataSource;
    }
}"},{"order":12,"type":"paragraph","text":"Similarly, the `ProdConfig` class and its `dataSource` bean will only be registered when the `prod` profile is active. This allows you to have different data source configurations for different environments."},{"order":13,"type":"subheading","text":"Activating Profiles"},{"order":14,"type":"paragraph","text":"There are several ways to activate Spring Profiles:"},{"order":15,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Using the `spring.profiles.active` property in `application.properties` or `application.yml`."},{"order":16,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Using the `SPRING_PROFILES_ACTIVE` environment variable."},{"order":17,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Using the `-Dspring.profiles.active` JVM argument."},{"order":18,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Programmatically using `ConfigurableEnvironment.setActiveProfiles()`."},{"order":19,"type":"subheading","text":"Using application.properties/application.yml"},{"order":20,"type":"paragraph","text":"The most common way to activate profiles in Spring Boot is by setting the `spring.profiles.active` property in your `application.properties` or `application.yml` file."},{"order":21,"type":"code","text":"# application.properties
spring.profiles.active=dev"},{"order":22,"type":"code","text":"# application.yml
spring:
  profiles:
    active: dev"},{"order":23,"type":"paragraph","text":"You can specify multiple active profiles by separating them with commas:"},{"order":24,"type":"code","text":"# application.properties
spring.profiles.active=dev,debug"},{"order":25,"type":"subheading","text":"Environment Variables"},{"order":26,"type":"paragraph","text":"You can also set the `SPRING_PROFILES_ACTIVE` environment variable to activate profiles. This is useful for configuring profiles in deployment environments."},{"order":27,"type":"code","text":"export SPRING_PROFILES_ACTIVE=prod"},{"order":28,"type":"subheading","text":"JVM Arguments"},{"order":29,"type":"paragraph","text":"You can also activate profiles by passing the `-Dspring.profiles.active` JVM argument when starting the application."},{"order":30,"type":"code","text":"java -Dspring.profiles.active=test -jar myapp.jar"},{"order":31,"type":"subheading","text":"Profile-Specific Configuration Files"},{"order":32,"type":"paragraph","text":"Spring Boot supports profile-specific configuration files. You can create files named `application-{profile}.properties` or `application-{profile}.yml` to provide configurations specific to a particular profile."},{"order":33,"type":"code","text":"# application-dev.properties
server.port=8081"},{"order":34,"type":"code","text":"# application-prod.properties
server.port=80"},{"order":35,"type":"paragraph","text":"In this example, when the `dev` profile is active, the application will run on port 8081, and when the `prod` profile is active, it will run on port 80."},{"order":36,"type":"subheading","text":"Example: Database Configuration"},{"order":37,"type":"paragraph","text":"Let's create a complete example with different database configurations for development, test, and production environments."},{"order":38,"type":"code","text":"// Development Configuration
@Configuration
@Profile("dev")
public class DevDataSourceConfig {

    @Bean
    @Primary
    public DataSource dataSource() {
        DriverManagerDataSource dataSource = new DriverManagerDataSource();
        dataSource.setDriverClassName("org.h2.Driver");
        dataSource.setUrl("jdbc:h2:mem:devdb;DB_CLOSE_DELAY=-1");
        dataSource.setUsername("sa");
        dataSource.setPassword("");
        return dataSource;
    }
}"},{"order":39,"type":"code","text":"// Test Configuration
@Configuration
@Profile("test")
public class TestDataSourceConfig {

    @Bean
    @Primary
    public DataSource dataSource() {
        DriverManagerDataSource dataSource = new DriverManagerDataSource();
        dataSource.setDriverClassName("org.h2.Driver");
        dataSource.setUrl("jdbc:h2:mem:testdb;DB_CLOSE_DELAY=-1");
        dataSource.setUsername("sa");
        dataSource.setPassword("");
        return dataSource;
    }
}"},{"order":40,"type":"code","text":"// Production Configuration
@Configuration
@Profile("prod")
public class ProdDataSourceConfig {

    @Bean
    @Primary
    public DataSource dataSource() {
        DriverManagerDataSource dataSource = new DriverManagerDataSource();
        dataSource.setDriverClassName("com.mysql.cj.jdbc.Driver");
        dataSource.setUrl("jdbc:mysql://production-db-url:3306/mydb");
        dataSource.setUsername("prod_user");
        dataSource.setPassword("prod_password");
        return dataSource;
    }
}"},{"order":41,"type":"paragraph","text":"In this example, we have three different configurations for the `DataSource` bean, each specific to a different profile. The `@Primary` annotation ensures that Spring will use this bean when a `DataSource` is required."},{"order":42,"type":"subheading","text":"Testing Profiles"},{"order":43,"type":"paragraph","text":"When writing tests, you can activate profiles using the `@ActiveProfiles` annotation."},{"order":44,"type":"code","text":"@SpringBootTest
@ActiveProfiles("test")
public class MyServiceTest {

    @Autowired
    private MyService myService;

    @Test
    public void testMyService() {
        // Your test logic here
    }
}"},{"order":45,"type":"paragraph","text":"This annotation ensures that the `test` profile is active during the test execution, allowing you to test the application with the test-specific configuration."},{"order":46,"type":"subheading","text":"Conclusion"},{"order":47,"type":"paragraph","text":"Spring Profiles are a powerful mechanism for managing different environments in your Spring Boot applications. By using profiles, you can easily switch between different configurations for development, testing, and production, making your application more flexible and maintainable."}]
